Abstract: This paper proposes allowing user-defined operator functions to declare additional trailing parameters with
Abstract: default arguments (beyond the fixed number of operands today), evaluated at the call site—mirroring the existing
Abstract: exception for `operator()`. The motivating use case is passing `std::source_location` into operators so
Abstract: implementations and diagnostics can record where `a @ b` was written without non-operator wrappers.
</pre>

Introduction {#intro}
============

Many libraries implement smart reference types, units, matrices, or logging proxies where user-defined operators (`+`,
`==`, subscripting, etc.) are the natural surface syntax. Such interfaces are primarily used, where operations are
heavily used across the source code, thus using named functions would result in excessive verbosty in reduction
of code readability. Within such use cases code monitoring and additional debugging capabilities are essential.

Authors often want **call-site provenance**: file, line, and column where the operator expression appeared.

C++20 provides <a href="https://wg21.link/source.location.syn">`std::source_location`</a> for that purpose on ordinary functions,
but operator invocations cannot carry a trailing `std::source_location` parameter today in a portable way while
preserving `a + b` syntax.

Problem {#problem}
=========

For a non-operator function, the idiom is well supported:

```cpp
void f(std::source_location loc = std::source_location::current());
```

For user-defined operators, the working draft states in <a
href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.oper.general">[over.oper.general]</a>:

> An operator function cannot have default arguments, except where explicitly stated below. Operator functions cannot
> have more or fewer parameters than the number required for the corresponding operator, as described in the rest of
> [over.oper].

That rule dissallows the usual `std::source_location::current()` trailing-parameter
idiom on overloads such as `operator+`, aside from carve-outs spelled out “below” in <a
href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.oper">[over.oper]</a> (notably `operator()`).

As a result, authors resort to named functions (`add(a, b)`) instead of operators (ergonomic loss).

Example:

```cpp
struct quantity {
  int value;
  friend constexpr quantity
  operator+(quantity a, quantity b,
             std::source_location where = std::source_location::current());
};

quantity x = a + b;
```

Using std::source_location within the operator itself will provide the source data of the operator implementation,
which is generally obvious and does not bring in new data (and could be workaround by good old #FILE and #LINE
macro definitions). Thus allowing std::source_location within the default value of the argument is
essential for the monitoring methodology to work.

We may collect usage statistics, based on caller context. E.g. reporting wrap-around
cases, as potential for usage error. But it is almost useless, unless we have data about the context
in which it happens.

Proposed direction {#proposal}
==================

The intent of the change is to treat user-defined operators like ordinary functions for **trailing parameters with default
arguments**, subject to:

1. The leading parameters still satisfy the operator’s required operand pattern (unary, binary, etc.) as today.
2. Additional parameters, if any, appear **only after** the operand parameters and **all** have default arguments.
3. Instantiate defaults in the **operator expression**, so `std::source_location::current()` captures the caller.

Note: the language could also allow extra operator parameters without defaults, callable only through explicit
`a.operator+(1, 2, 3)` syntax, but there is no clear use case for that broader generalization.

Alternatives considered {#alternics}
=======================

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Drawback</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Named APIs</td><td>Loses operator notation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>std::stacktrace</td>
        <td>Brings in a variety of requirements (for example RTTI) and is generally much heavier than
        <code>std::source_location</code>, as well as reporting granularity is implementation defined,
        thus we may not see the exact place of operator invocation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Compiler built-in</td><td>Running a built-in inside the operator body loses caller-site context and does not
        provide a satisfactory solution.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reflection</td><td>Does not appear to recover caller-site context for this problem.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Wording strategy {#wording_strategy}
==================

There are several operator kinds and several sections of the standard that describe how overload resolution works for
operators; those places could be affected by adding new parameters.

The general section for overloaded operators is <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.oper.general">[over.oper.general]</a>.

The key wording that forbids default arguments is:

<cite>"An operator function cannot have default arguments, except where explicitly stated below. Operator functions cannot
have more or fewer parameters than the number required for the corresponding operator, as described in the rest of [over.oper]."</cite>

Further rules are split across subclauses; the most general are unary operators
<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.unary">[over.unary]</a> and binary operators
<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.binary.general">[over.binary.general]</a>.
Today they are distinguished explicitly by the number of parameters in their member and non-member forms.

Once the operator’s kind is fixed, resolution is described under <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.match.oper">[over.match.oper]</a>,
which has several parts:

- the algorithm for populating the candidate sets should not be affected by the proposed change;
- choosing among candidates defers to the usual function rules (<a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.match.viable">[over.match.viable]</a>, <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.match.best">[over.match.best]</a>) and should likewise be unaffected.

This is followed by a set of dedicated sections:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Operator form</th>
      <th>Special considerations for trailing defaults</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Unary operators<br />
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.unary">[over.unary]</a></td>
      <td>Introduces the definition:<br />
      "either a non-static member function ([class.mfct]) with no non-object parameters or a non-member function with one parameter."<br /><br />
      Distinguishing unary and binary forms where both exist, for example <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>,
      and <code>&amp;</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>General binary operators<br />
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.binary.general">[over.binary.general]</a></td>
      <td>Introduces the definition:<br />
      "either a non-static member function ([class.mfct]) with one non-object parameter or a non-member function with two parameters."</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Simple assignment<br />
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.assign">[over.assign]</a></td>
      <td>Refers to the previous section (general binary operators) for the parameter list and its behavior.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Function call operator<br />
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.call">[over.call]</a></td>
      <td>Is already explicitly allowed to have default arguments:<br />
      <cite>"with an arbitrary number of parameters. It may have default arguments."</cite></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Subscripting operator<br />
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.sub">[over.sub]</a></td>
      <td>Is already explicitly allowed to have default arguments:<br />
      <cite>"with an arbitrary number of parameters. It may have default arguments."</cite>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Class member access operators<br />
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.ref">[over.ref]</a></td>
      <td>Forbids any non-object arguments. Can be extended to forbidding any non-object argument, unless they are defaulted.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Increment and decrement<br />
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.inc">[over.inc]</a></td>
      <td>Explicitly distinguishes the prefix case (no dummy parameter) from the postfix case (an extra parameter of
      type <code>int</code>). Could be extended to allow further parameters with default arguments.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The following overloadable operators have dedicated sections elsewhere in the draft.

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Operator form</th>
      <th>Special considerations for trailing defaults</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Dynamic storage duration. <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.stc.dynamic">[basic.stc.dynamic]</a><br />
      <code>new</code>, <code>new[]</code>, <code>delete</code>, and <code>delete[]</code> operators</td>
      <td>
      Is already implicitely allowed to have default arguments:

      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.stc.dynamic">[basic.stc.dynamic]</a> restricts first parameters to be of certain type
      and have no default values, but the rest can be extended with arbitrary arguments and can have default values.</td>

      The meaning of extended arguments and overload resolution is decribed further here: <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.new">[expr.new]</a> and
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.delete">[expr.delete]</a>. And it does not restrict those arguments to have default arguments.
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>operator co_await</code><br />
      Unary <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.await">[expr.await]</a></td>
      <td>The only place found that discusses the number of parameters on an overloaded <code>operator co_await</code> is:

      <cite>"o is determined by enumerating the applicable operator co_await functions for an argument a ([over.match.oper]),
      and choosing the best one through overload resolution ([over.match]). If overload resolution is ambiguous,
      the program is ill-formed. If no viable functions are found, o is a. Otherwise, o is a call to the selected
      function with the argument a. If o would be a prvalue, the temporary materialization conversion ([conv.rval]) is applied."</cite>

      This wording does not explicitly restrict the parameter count of the overloaded operator; it only describes how the
      single operand <code>a</code> participates in overload resolution.

      A quick check with GCC and Clang (<a href="https://godbolt.org/z/r5vMToj3K">Compiler Explorer</a>) shows both compilers
      rejecting extra parameters on <code>operator co_await</code> today.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Some frightening operators, which do not have specific sections for overloaded operators:

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Operator form</th>
      <th>Special considerations for trailing defaults</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>operator,</code></td>
      <td>The comma operator in <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.comma">[expr.comma]</a> does not introduce dedicated
      rules that would constrain the parameter list of an overloaded <code>operator,</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Indirection <code>operator*</code> and address-of <code>operator&amp;</code></td>
      <td>Defined in the unary operators clause <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.unary.op">[expr.unary.op]</a>; those
      rules do not appear to constrain defaulted trailing parameters if they were introduced.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Comparisons</td>
      <td>Described in <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.spaceship">[expr.spaceship]</a> (three-way comparison operator),
      <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.rel">[expr.rel]</a> (relational operators), and <a
      href="https://eel.is/c++draft/class.spaceship">[class.spaceship]</a> (including <cite>synthesized three-way comparison</cite>).

      None of these sections appear to interfere with defaulted trailing parameters on the underlying comparison operators,
      and they should not block the current proposal.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Wording {#wording}
=======

It looks sufficient to address the following aspects:
1. Remove phrasing the explicitely disallows to have argument with default values for operators.
2. Modify phrasing, that Unary operato definition may imply additional arguments, if they have default values.
3. Same for binary operators.
4. Additional wording will be required to allow defulat arguments for increments/decrements and class member access operators.
5. Optionaly, explicitely allow (or may by disallow) additional arguments for <code>operator co_await</code>.

No formal drafting is included in **R0**.

Implementation notes {#impl}
======================

Draft implementations will follow once R0 shows there is no strong opposition to the proposed direction.
